text:
" An' when the old folks die
And no one is left to cry,
I will be there,
Just to see their grave;
To tell them then that they were loved.

But if you ask my reason why,
My dearie, this heart won't say;
For if you asked it now,
It would surely go low ;
An' then I know we should never meet again."

I shall not ask for any treasure,
 Only a gift from thee ;
 A garden where there is no sorrow;
 Where the heart need never grieve;
 No care with its burden pressing,
 No pain that can be seen,
 Nor any thought which will oppress me ;
 For it cannot come between !

There is a tree on which are hanging,
 All the fruits that grow at home;
 There are leaves that drop in showers,
 With every gusty wind that blows;
 And there are flowers for each month in the year,
 That open as they fall below;
 But this only pleases me best of all, --

There came a little vessel from the West;
It was so small we could not see it rest:
Some sailors spoke their greetings as they went;
They wished me joy of my young mistress’s rent;
The girl was in her chamber just at hand.
We sat upon the beach till day appeared;
Then she took off her mourning for my sake;
I stood, I thought, on hallowed ground before;
She would have plucked some flowers but could not bear
To touch a blossom growing in our way,
For fear of troubling God, who knows all

I’m an old man now, with little hope in life;
The world has turned against us all, so it seems;
We had our time when we were young and strong;
But age is marching on, leaving no gleam.

There is one thing though I must say; I have loved the ocean always. .
When youth was fresh and my hopes bright,
I dreamed of sailing the wide blue sea,
Where the waves rolled in beauty’s light,
All over the face of this earth—

And now, as my strength ebbs away,

The sun came up like thunder
 And there was an endless sound;
 The wind went sighing through the reeds,
 As if it would never cease;
 There were great waves all around.
